#once

//--the base I6 object; supports a name, attributes, and placement in the world map
extern class object:_bglObject{ 
    //
    typesealed parentProp parent;
    typesealed childrenProp children;       
    typesealed attributeList attributes; 
    
    emitter void give(attribute attr){ give $val $attr }
    
    emitter void ungive(attribute attr){ give $val ~$attr }
    /// True when this object has `attr`.
    emitter bool has(attribute attr){
        ($val has $attr)
    }
    /// True when this object does not have `attr`.
    emitter bool hasnt(attribute attr){
        ($val hasnt $attr)
    }
    /// Take this object out of the world tree. Its members are untouched; `.parent = X` puts it back.
    emitter void remove(){
        remove $val
    }
    /// This object's first child, or `nothing`. For all of them, iterate `.children`.
    emitter object child{
        child($val)
    }
    /// The next object under the same parent, or `nothing`.
    emitter object sibling{
        sibling($val)
    }
}

//--setters
/// The type of `object.parent`. Assigning to it moves the object in the world tree.
extern emitter class parentProp{   //obj.parent=loc; -> move obj to loc;
    emitter parentProp operator = (object v){
        move $self to $v
    }
    emitter eBool operator == (object v){
        parent($self) == $v
    }
    emitter eBool operator != (object v){
        parent($self) ~= $v
    }
    emitter object operator(){
        parent($self)
    }
}

//--the world-tree child collection. `obj.children` is iterable (the compiler lowers
//  `for(o in obj.children)` to an I6 objectloop); placement (`children = {a,b}` in a body,
//  `.children += {x}` at runtime) is compiler-tracked. `length()`/`size()` report the child
//  count — routines like `<array>`'s, using `$host` (the object the proxy member is read on,
//  i.e. `obj` in `obj.children`) since the proxy has no storage of its own. size()==length()
//  here: a world-tree collection has no capacity-vs-in-use distinction.
/// The type of `object.children` — the world-tree child collection.
extern emitter class childrenProp{
    /// How many children this object has.
    emitter int length(){ children($host) }
    /// How many children this object has. Same as `length()`: a world-tree collection has no spare capacity.
    emitter int size()  { children($host) }
}


/*
then we can add some follow ups...  

we now have clear copy and ref semantics.  But...

Some types make more sense to pass by reference by default.  For
example, copying a room makes little sense, so making ref be declared is burdensome.

Another thought: copy semantics don't cover all values automatically, since new properties can be declared 
on an object.

So maybe we still need some work to do on the byRef, byVal concepts.

Also: classes using root assignment behave as byRef, even though they aren't specified that way feels
like a bit of hidden logic.  We could fire an error, like we do for ref, an object-copy expected value can't 
be ref-copied (counterpoint to the ref rule).

I'm coming around to the idea of identifying a class as ref or val, possibly the rule we just removed.  But I'd
need clear understanding about what the identifier did. For example, if it is marked as default ref, would 
we even allow copy operators?

okay.  three new types?  ref value and primitive.  Primitive forces the value behavior, and blocks the ref behavior.  Right?  


